fers  people something that they really do  need but if we want to compete with that  we have to say as you say it's an  experience you come here and you get  something completely different than if  you're clicking online because I shop  online but I also support local  businesses I would never even dream of  say buying my meat or my vegetables  online because I've got a market on my  doorstep maybe that's the key town  centers have to be about more than just  shopping  perhaps it has to become more well fun  this site used to be the Danka ster  branch of BHS department store it's been  transformed into a trampolining and  activity center all in the middle of a  shopping now it's a question of looking  at perhaps looking at the opportunity  and thinking you know what service can I  provide or what goods and services can I  provide that Amazon can't that main  keeps coming up amazon isn't the only  online giant in fact it only accounts  for 2% of retail sales in this country  but it has become a byword for online  shopping and also a symbol of how the  traditional retail world is being  morphed by global companies with opaque  tax arrangements we pay all of our taxes  that we are expected to pay we followed  the rules and we always will do that  whether there is a question around a  future tax or change to tax is a  question which is best answered by the  politicians Britain shopkeepers and  their communities do face challenging  times technology is racing forward as  we've revealed the high street has  reached its peak it will change and  become smaller but it won't disappear  like Doncaster itself retailers will  just need to embrace the art of  reinvention  Adam Parsons Sky News Doncaster
 

